- TA0004 - Privilege Escalation
  -- T1548 - Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism
    --- T1548.002 - Bypass User Account Control

# THREAT DESCRIPTION

  Adversaries may abuse Windows User Account Control (UAC) mechanisms to execute processes with elevated privileges without following the expected elevation workflow.
  UAC helps limit the privileges available to processes until an authorized elevation occurs.
  Attackers may exploit trusted Windows applications, auto-elevation behavior, user-writable registry locations, or elevated COM interfaces to obtain a higher-integrity execution context.
  Certain UAC bypass techniques involve modifying per-user registry protocol handlers and subsequently launching trusted Windows binaries such as fodhelper.exe or computerdefaults.exe.
  These registry modifications may redirect execution toward attacker-controlled commands or payloads.
  Other UAC bypass methods may rely on DLL hijacking, COM object abuse, application-specific behavior, or vulnerabilities.
  Successful exploitation may result in a High Integrity process executing commands, modifying protected system resources, disabling security controls, or establishing additional persistence.
  Legitimate elevated applications and administrative workflows may exhibit overlapping characteristics, making registry, process lineage, integrity level, and execution context correlation essential.

# THREAT MODEL

  - Attacker Goal:
      Obtain elevated process execution by bypassing or abusing Windows UAC mechanisms

  - Prerequisites:
      Ability to execute code on the target Windows endpoint
      Access to an applicable UAC bypass mechanism
      Sufficient permissions to modify the relevant user-writable registry locations when registry hijacking is involved
      A security context and Windows configuration in which the selected bypass technique is applicable
      An administrator account operating with a filtered token for common auto-elevation bypass scenarios

  - Potential Impact:
      Privilege Escalation
      Elevated Command Execution
      Defense Evasion
      Security Control Modification
      Persistence Establishment
      Credential Access
      Further Endpoint Compromise

