# DETECTION STRATEGY

  Detection relies on identifying suspicious modifications to registry locations associated with UAC bypass mechanisms and correlating those modifications with trusted auto-elevated process execution.
  The initial Sigma rule focuses on monitored modifications to the per-user ms-settings shell open command handler.
  Registry modification telemetry provides the initial detection signal, while process creation, integrity level, token information, and registry value data provide supporting evidence.
  SIEM correlation should determine whether the modified registry configuration was followed by execution of a relevant trusted Windows binary and an unexpected elevated payload.
  The strategy prioritizes multi-event execution chains rather than treating all elevated processes or all registry handler modifications as malicious.
  High-confidence alerts require reliable linkage between suspicious configuration changes and elevated execution, or other strong evidence of unauthorized privilege elevation.
  Other UAC bypass methods involving COM interfaces, DLL hijacking, or different registry handlers require broader correlation logic or additional dedicated detections.

# DETECTION LOGIC NOTES

  - UAC bypass techniques vary significantly across Windows versions and security configurations.
  - Common auto-elevation bypass techniques may require an administrator account operating with a filtered token.
  - UAC bypass should not be interpreted as automatically granting administrator privileges to an otherwise unprivileged account.
  - High Integrity execution alone does not prove UAC bypass.
  - The absence of an observed UAC prompt is not sufficient evidence unless reliable prompt or consent telemetry is available.
  - Registry modification and successful privilege elevation are separate detection conditions.
  - The per-user ms-settings handler may appear under HKCU or HKU\<UserSID> depending on the telemetry source.
  - Registry paths and value names should be normalized before correlation.
  - Sysmon Event ID 13 records registry value modifications but does not independently confirm that the configured command executed.
  - Sysmon Event ID 12 can provide supporting key creation or deletion evidence.
  - The registry default value may be represented as (Default) or another normalized value name depending on the telemetry pipeline.
  - An empty DelegateExecute value can be relevant to known UAC bypass techniques.
  - The existence of DelegateExecute alone does not confirm malicious activity.
  - The configured default command may reference an interpreter, script, executable, or indirect command.
  - Registry value data should be parsed and compared with subsequent execution telemetry.
  - ProcessGuid and ParentProcessGuid should be preferred for direct process lineage.
  - Suspicious execution may occur through indirect or multi-level process ancestry.
  - Integrity levels should be evaluated alongside user identity, token information, and process lineage.
  - Legitimate administrative elevation can produce Medium-to-High Integrity execution sequences.
  - Registry cleanup following suspicious execution may be relevant but is not independent proof of malicious activity.
  - Registry modifications by legitimate installers or approved administrative tools may produce false positives.
  - Exclusions should be narrowly scoped to validated benign registry modification behavior.
  - Execution of fodhelper.exe or computerdefaults.exe shortly after a registry modification does not independently confirm UAC bypass.
  - Correlation should verify the affected user context, registry value data, process lineage, and subsequent elevated execution before attributing activity to the bypass mechanism.
  - The Sigma rule covers a specific registry-based UAC bypass pattern rather than all T1548.002 techniques.
  - Detection quality depends on registry telemetry, process creation visibility, integrity level information, and retained execution history.

