# LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS

  The Sigma rule provides Medium-priority detection of registry modifications associated with known UAC bypass mechanisms.
  A registry modification alone does not establish successful privilege escalation.
  Legitimate applications and administrative tools may modify registry protocol handlers.
  High-confidence detection requires additional evidence such as trusted auto-elevated binary execution followed by unexpected elevated payload execution.
  Integrity level information should support correlation but should not be used as independent proof of malicious elevation.
  Consequently, Sigma severity is Medium while correlated alert severity may range from High to Critical depending on the observed execution chain and subsequent activity.

# IMPLEMENTATION NOTES

  The Sigma rule represents the initial registry modification detection layer for selected UAC bypass techniques.
  Organizations should validate the relevant registry paths and value naming conventions against their Windows telemetry.
  Registry telemetry should capture modifications to per-user ms-settings shell open command values.
  HKCU and HKU\<UserSID> representations should be normalized for reliable correlation.
  Registry value data should be retained to identify the configured command or executable.
  Process creation telemetry should include Image, CommandLine, ParentImage, ProcessGuid, ParentProcessGuid, User, and IntegrityLevel when available.
  Process lineage should be reconstructed using stable process identifiers.
  SIEM correlation should verify whether a relevant trusted Windows binary executed after the registry modification.
  Subsequent elevated process execution should be matched against the configured registry command when possible.
  User SID, token context, and integrity level information should be used to distinguish expected administrative elevation from suspicious behavior.
  The Sigma rule is intentionally limited to the ms-settings shell open command handler.
  Other UAC bypass methods require additional detection logic and should not be considered directly covered by this Sigma rule.
  Underlying registry and process telemetry should remain available for SIEM correlation even when an event does not match the Sigma rule.
  Approved administrative and software configuration activity should be evaluated before introducing narrowly scoped exclusions.
  High-confidence alert generation should be performed through SIEM-side enrichment and correlation between:

    - Registry Modification Events
    - Registry Key Creation and Deletion Events
    - Process Creation Events
    - Process Integrity Level Telemetry
    - Process Token Elevation Telemetry
